---
title: Overview
description: How invoices are raised from CRM, issued, sent to the client and followed until they are paid.
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

Invoices are raised in CRM, either from a signed contract or directly for a client. Each invoice is edited on a full-page editor that shows the invoice exactly as the client will see it. When the invoice is issued, it takes its number and its PDF is filed. It is then sent to the client by email with a link to an online copy, and followed until it is paid. Corrections are made with credit notes and by voiding, never by deleting an issued invoice.

## Where invoices are found

| Place | What it shows |
|---|---|
| **Invoices** in the left sidebar | Every invoice and credit note of the firm. |
| The opportunity › **Invoices** tab | The invoices of one deal. The tab comes right after the **Contract** tab. |
| The company or contact › **Invoices** tab | The invoices of one client. |

All three show the same list, with the same tabs, columns and actions. A new invoice can be started from any of them with **+ New invoice**; on the firm-wide page you first choose the client.

## The life of an invoice

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **Draft** | Being prepared. A draft has no number and is not sent to anyone. It can be changed or deleted. |
| **Awaiting payment** | Issued: it has a number and can be sent. Nothing has been paid yet. |
| **Partially paid** | Part of the amount has been received or covered by a credit note. |
| **Paid** | Nothing remains due. |
| **Void** | Cancelled with a reason. It keeps its number, and its online link stops working. |
| **Overdue** | Shown for an invoice that is awaiting payment or partially paid after its due date. |

A credit note shows **Credit remaining** while part of its credit is still unused, and **Fully applied** when all of it is used. An overpayment, the money a client paid beyond an invoice's amount due, uses the same two statuses (see [Overpayments](/en/invoices/overpayments/)).

## The issuing entity

Every invoice is issued by an issuing entity: the firm's company that signs the contract. An invoice created from a contract takes the contract's issuing entity. The issuing entity decides the GST treatment, the bank account printed on the invoice and the number series. Two issuing entities number their invoices separately.

An issuing entity can be linked to a Xero organisation. Its invoices and credit notes are then pushed to Xero when they are issued, take their numbers from Xero's sequence, and have their payments recorded in Xero (see [Connect Xero](/en/invoices/settings-reminders/#connect-xero)).

## Who does what

| Role | Work |
|---|---|
| **Member** (any staff member who can see the client or the opportunity) | Creates, edits and deletes drafts. Issues invoices, sends them by email, copies the online link, duplicates invoices and records payments. Adds notes. |
| **Manager** | Everything a member does. Also voids invoices, creates, issues and applies credit notes, applies overpayments, records refunds, deletes payments, removes credit allocations, and maintains the invoice templates. |
| **Tenant admin** | Everything a manager does. Also maintains the invoice settings, the settings of each issuing entity, turns automatic payment reminders on and sets their rules, and connects Xero. Only tenant admins can open the invoice settings. |

An action that a role can never take is not shown to that role. An action that is shown but cannot be taken yet is greyed out, with the reason written under it.

